#      1:     78: ! * Note for the implementation.                                                 *
#      1:     79: ! * ===========================                                                  *
#      1:     80: ! * This interpreter is written for the case where all the fundamental types     *
#      1:     81: ! * take one storage unit.                                                       *
#      1:     82: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:     83: ! * In an actual implementation, the handling of the sp pointer has to take      *
#      1:     84: ! * into account the fact that the types may have lengths different from one:    *
#      1:     85: ! * in push and pop operations the sp has to be increased and decreased not      *
#      1:     86: ! * by 1, but by a number depending on the type concerned.                       *
#      1:     87: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:     88: ! * However, where the number of units of storage has been computed by the       *
#      1:     89: ! * compiler, the value must not be corrected, since the lengths of the types    *
#      1:     90: ! * involved have already been taken into account.                               *
#      1:     91: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:     92: ! * P5 errors added:                                                             *
#      1:     93: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:     94: ! * 182 identifier too long                                                      *
#      1:     95: ! * 183 For index variable must be local to this block                           *
#      1:     96: ! * 184 Interprocedure goto does not reference outter block of destination       *
#      1:     97: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:     98: ! * P5 instructions modified:                                                    *
#      1:     99: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:    100: ! * lca'string'       '                                                          *
#      1:    101: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:    102: ! * was changed to                                                               *
#      1:    103: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:    104: ! * lca 'string'''                                                               *
#      1:    105: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:    106: ! * That is, lca has a space before the opening quote, no longer pads to the     *
#      1:    107: ! * right, and represents single quotes with a quote image. pint converts quote  *
#      1:    108: ! * images back to single quotes, and pads out strings to their full length.     *
#      1:    109: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:    110: ! * In addition, the way files work was extensively modified. Original P5 could  *
#      1:    111: ! * not represent files as fully expressed variables, such as within an array    *
#      1:    112: ! * or record, and were effectively treated as constants. To treat them as true  *
#      1:    113: ! * variable accesses, the stacking order of the file in all file subroutines    *
#      1:    114: ! * was changed so that the file is on the bottom. This matches the source       *
#      1:    115: ! * order of the file in write(f, ...) or read(f, ...). Also, the file           *
#      1:    116: ! * operations now leave the file on the stack for the duration of a write or    *
#      1:    117: ! * read, then dump them using a specific new instruction "dmp". This allows     *
#      1:    118: ! * multiparameter writes and reads to be effectively a chain of single          *
#      1:    119: ! * operations using one file reference. Finally, files were tied to the type    *
#      1:    120: ! * ending 'a', because files are now full variable references.                  *
#      1:    121: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:    122: ! * Layout of memory in store:                                                   *
#      1:    123: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:    124: ! *    maxstr ->    ---------------------                                        *
#      1:    125: ! *                 | Stack             |                                        *
#      1:    126: ! *        sp ->    ---------------------                                        *
#      1:    127: ! *                 | Free space        |                                        *
#      1:    128: ! *        np ->    ---------------------                                        *
#      1:    129: ! *                 | Heap              |                                        *
#      1:    130: ! *        gbtop -> ---------------------                                        *
#      1:    131: ! *                 | Globals           |                                        *
#      1:    132: ! *        pctop -> ---------------------                                        *
#      1:    133: ! *                 | Constants         |                                        *
#      1:    134: ! *                 ---------------------                                        *
#      1:    135: ! *                 | Code              |                                        *
#      1:    136: ! *                 ---------------------                                        *
#      1:    137: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:    138: ! * The constants are loaded upside down from the top of memory. The heap grows  *
#      1:    139: ! * down, the stack grows up, and when they cross, it is an overflow error.      *
#      1:    140: ! *                                                                              *
#      1:    141: ! * This is the mach (machine) module. This is a cut down interpreter derived    *
#      1:    142: ! * from pint, whose only job is to interpret the code. It loads from prd in     *
#      1:    143: ! * binary deck form, then interprets the code.                                  *
